declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-101';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 5, $j$2.1 Conquest: Joshua$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Joshua 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Joshua/1"}, {"title": "Joshua 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Joshua/3"}, {"title": "Joshua 6 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Joshua/6"}, {"title": "Joshua 24 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Joshua/24"}], "body": "Read Joshua 1, 3, 6 and 24 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nJoshua was Moses' helper and the man God chose to lead Israel across the Jordan River into the promised land. God told him to be strong and courageous and to meditate on the book of the law day and night. Israel crossed the Jordan on dry ground, the walls of Jericho fell after the people marched around the city for seven days, and the tribes came to possess the land one region at a time. The land was then divided among the twelve tribes. At the end of his life Joshua called the nation to choose whom they would serve.\n\nKey words: conquest, inheritance, courage, obedience, choose.\n\nYour task: (1) Name the river Israel crossed to enter the land. (2) Tell how the walls of Jericho fell. (3) Write Joshua 24:15 in your own words and say what choice Joshua made for his house.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Welcome back. Unit 1 gave us the Law. Now we turn to the historical books, and the first of them is Joshua. It picks up the story right where Deuteronomy ends. Moses is dead, the people stand on the east side of the Jordan River, and God speaks to a new leader.\n\nGod told Joshua, Have not I commanded thee? Be strong and of a good courage; be not afraid, neither be thou dismayed: for the LORD thy God is with thee whithersoever thou goest. That is Joshua 1:9. Notice the reason for courage. It is not that Joshua was strong. It is that the LORD was with him.\n\nThe people crossed the Jordan on dry ground, just as their parents had crossed the Red Sea. The priests carried the ark of the covenant into the water, and the river stopped. God was showing the new generation that the same God who delivered their fathers was still with them.\n\nThen came Jericho, a walled city that stood in their way. God gave an unusual battle plan. For six days the army marched around the city once a day, and on the seventh day they marched around seven times, the priests blew the trumpets, the people shouted, and the walls fell flat. The victory came by obedience and trust, not by a siege.\n\nThe rest of the book tells how the tribes took possession of the land and how it was divided among them. Each tribe received an inheritance, a place that God had promised to Abraham centuries before. Joshua 21:45 says, There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken unto the house of Israel; all came to pass. God keeps His promises.\n\nNear the end of his life Joshua gathered the nation and gave them a choice. Joshua 24:15 says, choose you this day whom ye will serve; but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD. Every person and every family has to answer that question.\n\nYour task is to name the river, tell how Jericho fell, and put Joshua 24:15 in your own words. Then take the four question check.\n\nCourage comes from knowing God is with you. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$2.1 Conquest: Joshua$j$);
end
$seed$;
